      "https://media.rawg.io/media/screenshots/588/588901dd579d3eca538912d1608530da.jpg",
      "https://media.rawg.io/media/screenshots/0de/0de9b078c2a17cf877336d546bdb5fb3.jpg",
      "https://media.rawg.io/media/screenshots/eb1/eb11fabddd43953d90ef46629f65a748.jpg",
      "https://media.rawg.io/media/screenshots/e96/e96943d97bd19325d03fbeb2c2e8e2d5.jpg",
      "https://media.rawg.io/media/screenshots/bd5/bd5e056e26b430bb560b167238f6fc56.jpg",
      "https://media.rawg.io/media/screenshots/270/2707dd5c0f261ec61a514341ce5a9121.jpg"
    ],
    "summary": "Mad Nurse is a video game programmed by Simon Pick and published by Firebird Software Ltd. for the Commodore 64 and ZX Spectrum in 1986.\nThe plot of the game is that you control a nurse at a maternity hospital, where you have to rescue the babies who have escaped from their cots. If left alone, the babies will eat medicine bottles, electrocute themselves at electrical outlets, and fall down the elevator shaft. The gameplay has the form of a non-scrolling platformer where the nurse can use an elevator to ascend or descend platforms. If too many babies die, the nurse is fired.\nFirebird initially refused to publish the game, because dying babies were too controversial, but changed their minds when their post of head of development changed hands. Some outlets also refused to sell the game.\nThe",
    "developer": "Telecomsoft",
    "reviews": []
  },
